WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:04.990
If you haven't seen Deadpool, but you like
what you see from Deadpool 2's amazing marketing

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:07.609
campaign, then you should probably go rent
Deadpool.

00:00:07.609 --> 00:00:10.240
It's pretty hilarious, but it's not for kids.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:14.540
Even though the sequel is only the second
movie in the series, it's a part of the long-running,

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:16.850
extra-confusing X-Men franchise.

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:21.360
Making matters even crazier, Deadpool 2 is
bringing in a ton of extra heroes from the

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:22.360
comics.

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:25.610
If you're planning to see the Merc with a
Mouth in action, but just don't have the time

00:00:25.610 --> 00:00:29.800
for Deadpool, or maybe you just want to know
who all these new people are, here's everything

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:32.850
you need to know before seeing Deadpool 2.

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:34.469
Mr. Pool

00:00:34.469 --> 00:00:38.829
Deadpool is Wade Wilson, a mercenary with
a military black ops past.

00:00:38.829 --> 00:00:42.840
The first job we see him work is scaring a
nerdy pizza delivery guy out of stalking the

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:44.370
object of his obsession.

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:49.250
He uses the money he gets from the gig for
a night of fun with his soon to be main squeeze,

00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:50.250
Vanessa.

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:54.739
Wilson and Vanessa spend a wild year together
until Wilson learns he's dying from cancer.

00:00:54.739 --> 00:00:59.180
With no other options available, Wilson agrees
to undergo an experimental treatment that

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:01.480
he's told will turn him into a superhero.

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:06.110
He doesn't learn until later that the program's
patients are actually super-powered slaves

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:08.180
sold off to the highest bidder.

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:12.460
The experiments give Wilson the ability to
heal from just about any wound, but they also

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:15.000
make his skin look like Freddy Krueger's.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:19.390
On his off time, Wilson frequents a bar called
"Sister Margaret's" that's patronized mainly

00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:20.390
by mercenaries.

00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:24.770
The bar's owner, Weasel, hosts a Dead Pool
in which the rowdy customers can bet on who

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:28.590
among them will die next, which inspires Wilson's
code name.

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:31.160
Deadpool's staple move is breaking the fourth
wall.

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:34.060
He knows he's a fictional character, and he
revels in it.

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:38.790
His movie takes plenty of potshots at other
superhero movies, particularly the X-Men series.

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:45.040
The character also pokes fun at Reynolds himself,
through jokes about his performances in Green

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:48.440
Lantern and X-Men Origins: Wolverine.

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:49.729
The cable guy

00:01:49.729 --> 00:01:54.340
There was a time in the '90s when it seemed
like just about every new X-Men character

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:57.649
was from some kind of dystopian alternate
future.

00:01:57.649 --> 00:02:00.380
Cable is one of these time-crossed refugees.

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:05.920
Played by Josh Brolin, Cable is Nathan Summers,
the child of Cyclops and a clone of Jean Grey

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:11.000
who's sent into the future and returns, initially
to take over command of the X-Men's junior

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:12.690
team, the New Mutants.

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:17.470
Cable's cybernetic enhancements are due to
a lethal techno-organic virus slowly turning

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:19.300
his body into a machine.

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:23.870
Luckily, he's also a mutant with telepathic
and telekinetic powers, which he uses to keep

00:02:23.870 --> 00:02:25.360
the virus at bay.

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:29.750
That's the Cable of the comics, but we don't
know how different the Cable of Deadpool 2

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:35.280
will be, just that he'll be from the future,
he's a cyborg, and he's a formidable warrior.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:39.480
We also know that Cable has come to the present
to with his sights on a teenager named Russell

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:41.090
for reasons unknown.

00:02:41.090 --> 00:02:42.780
Speaking of whom...

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:43.780
The new kid

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:47.540
The kid that Cable's hunting in the trailers
remains shrouded in mystery.

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:52.260
We know his name is Russell, and that he seems
to have pyrokinetic powers, giving us a solid

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:54.810
idea of his comic inspiration.

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:59.459
Russell is likely inspired by Rusty Collins,
a.k.a. Firefist.

00:02:59.459 --> 00:03:04.600
First appearing in the pages of X-Factor,
he's able to manipulate fire, much like Pyro

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:06.110
from X-Men 2.

00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:08.610
What we still don't know is what Cable wants
with him.

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:11.990
Does Russell somehow cause the dark future
that Cable's from?

00:03:11.990 --> 00:03:14.840
Or is this solely a mission of personal vengeance?

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:19.489
Either way, Deadpool takes up the cause of
defending the kid, assembling a new team to

00:03:19.489 --> 00:03:20.830
take Cable out.

00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:23.349
The luckiest lady

00:03:23.349 --> 00:03:28.830
Neena Thurman, a.k.a. Domino, is the end result
of a government breeding project designed

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:30.760
to produce the perfect killer.

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:36.569
Domino is surrounded at all times by a "probability
field" that affects her and her enemies.

00:03:36.569 --> 00:03:40.530
Try to shoot at Domino, and there's a good
chance your gun will jam, or even explode

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:41.670
in your face.

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:46.569
If Domino leaps from the top of a tall building
with no plan of survival, something is likely

00:03:46.569 --> 00:03:49.640
to come along and cushion or stop her fall.

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:54.239
Much like Deadpool, Domino's relationships
with Marvel's other mutants tend to be somewhat

00:03:54.239 --> 00:03:55.239
tenuous.

00:03:55.239 --> 00:03:59.379
Unlike the X-Men, she's no moral crusader,
but she'll often work alongside them if there's

00:03:59.379 --> 00:04:02.270
an opportunity for fun, profit, or both.

00:04:02.270 --> 00:04:06.870
She first appeared in the pages of the X-Men
comic X-Force, making her a natural fit for

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:10.209
the version of the team assembling in Deadpool
2.

00:04:10.209 --> 00:04:11.689
Pure bedlam

00:04:11.689 --> 00:04:17.079
Introduced during 1995's alternate universe
Age of Apocalypse event, Bedlam was originally

00:04:17.079 --> 00:04:21.750
the collective name of two brothers: Jesse
and Christopher Aaronson.

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:27.310
After that story wrapped up, Jesse Aaronson
was re-introduced in the pages of X-Force.

00:04:27.310 --> 00:04:32.330
Bedlam produces an electromagnetic field that
allows him to disrupt different forms of energy,

00:04:32.330 --> 00:04:34.919
creating havoc in both people and machines.

00:04:34.919 --> 00:04:36.889
He's also a martial arts expert.

00:04:36.889 --> 00:04:41.360
Again, there's no way to know until opening
day how faithful Terry Crews' portrayal will

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:45.970
be to the comics; only that, like his comic
book counterpart, Bedlam will be joining the

00:04:45.970 --> 00:04:48.180
ranks of X-Force.

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:50.169
Who is Shatterstar?

00:04:50.169 --> 00:04:54.900
If you think Cable's origin story is convoluted,
wait till you meet Shatterstar.

00:04:54.900 --> 00:05:00.030
In the comics, Shatterstar is from a place
called Mojoworld, a planet that exists a century

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:03.630
in the future and is ruled by the tyrannical
villain Mojo.

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:09.000
Mojoworld is obsessed with entertainment,
and Shatterstar was a slave who was genetically

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:10.920
engineered to be a gladiator.

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:16.199
He has enhanced strength and agility, heals
faster than most, is a master at many different

00:05:16.199 --> 00:05:21.350
fighting styles, can channel shockwaves through
weaponry, and can even open portals allowing

00:05:21.350 --> 00:05:24.290
people to instantly teleport to wherever they'd
like.

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:28.600
As far as his origin in Deadpool 2 is concerned,
it's likely it will have nothing to do with

00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:29.730
Mojoworld.

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:33.810
But considering how much Deadpool made fun
of comic book movie tropes, who knows?

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:38.820
We could have a scene with Cable, Bedlam,
and Shatterstar all comparing dystopian futures,

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:41.009
debating whose is worse.

00:05:41.009 --> 00:05:42.789
Peter and Hydra Bob

00:05:42.789 --> 00:05:43.789
Who is Peter?

00:05:43.789 --> 00:05:46.220
Well, the short answer is that we don't know.

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:51.530
There's some speculation that he's Deadpool
2's answer to longtime Deadpool comics character

00:05:51.530 --> 00:05:52.759
Hydra Bob.

00:05:52.759 --> 00:05:57.509
Hydra Bob is, on the surface, a kind of useless
sidekick to Deadpool.

00:05:57.509 --> 00:06:02.050
He's cowardly, bad at fighting, and has absolutely
no special skills.

00:06:02.050 --> 00:06:07.220
He's a member of Hydra who will often cite
Hydra training maxims, such as "if I can't

00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:10.770
see them they can't see me" and "hiding behind
each other."

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:14.280
He generally responds to every situation by
running away.

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:17.830
The character actually had an unofficial cameo
in Deadpool.

00:06:17.830 --> 00:06:22.310
He's never referred to as "Hydra Bob" because
20th Century Fox didn't have the rights to

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:26.520
that character, but Deadpool calls him Bob
and they briefly talk about working together

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:27.810
in Jacksonville.

00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:32.069
He's among the only ones of Ajax's henchmen
that Deadpool knocks unconscious and doesn't

00:06:32.069 --> 00:06:32.740
kill.

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:43.160
It's possible that Fox wanted a Hydra Bob
for Deadpool 2, and simply created their own

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:46.550
schlub with a different name because they
didn't have the rights.

00:06:46.550 --> 00:06:48.139
Not that Marvel universe

00:06:48.139 --> 00:06:53.880
Though Deadpool is a Marvel Comics character,
he doesn't live anywhere near the mostly family-friendly,

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:56.069
Disney-owned Marvel Cinematic Universe.

00:06:56.069 --> 00:07:00.380
Though Deadpool is not technically a mutant,
he's one of the many Marvel characters owned

00:07:00.380 --> 00:07:06.319
by 20th Century Fox as part of the movie rights
to the X-Men's roster of heroes and villains.

00:07:06.319 --> 00:07:13.889
So Deadpool doesn't have to worry about going
up against Thanos anytime soon.

00:07:13.889 --> 00:07:18.289
Disney is currently in the process of buying
and merging with Fox, meaning that actual

00:07:18.289 --> 00:07:21.139
crossovers will likely be possible in the
future.

00:07:21.139 --> 00:07:25.729
But until then, Deadpool is most definitely
banned from Disney World.

00:07:25.729 --> 00:07:26.890
Thanks for watching!

00:07:26.890 --> 00:07:29.419
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:29.419 --> 00:07:31.640
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

